\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two choices for the third direction}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take #{X,Y} of \ref{fdip-017M} as the first two directions
on #{\mathbb C^2}. Their even grading is #{\ui XY=-Z}.
Either choice of #{Z} or #{-Z} for the third direction is a
self-adjoint involution anticommuting with both. In
\ref{fdip-017N} the choice #{-Z} has #{\sigma=1}, and #{Z}
has #{\sigma=-1}. Any linear intertwiner of the first two
directions commutes with the whole matrix algebra and is scalar.
It cannot interchange these opposite third directions on one
copy. Both triples act on the same two-dimensional space.}
